cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use fishsuite in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "fishsuite: a Python pipeline for RNA-FISH and immunofluorescence quantification and colocalization"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Amburn
    given-names: Brian
    email: gsdewson@utmb.edu
    affiliation: "Department of Biochemistry & Molecular Biology, University of Texas Medical Branch, Galveston, TX, USA"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4977-5475"
repository-code: "https://github.com/SpaceSorcerer/fishsuite"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
keywords:
  - microscopy
  - RNA-FISH
  - immunofluorescence
  - colocalization
  - image analysis
  - single-molecule FISH
  - nuclei segmentation
  - spot detection
abstract: >-
  fishsuite quantifies single-molecule RNA-FISH and immunofluorescence confocal
  images: nuclei segmentation (StarDist, Cellpose, or Otsu), spot detection
  (Big-FISH Laplacian-of-Gaussian or plain LoG), subcellular compartment
  assignment, and colocalization between two channels. Colocalization is
  reported both as threshold-free pixel statistics (Pearson, Spearman, Manders,
  Li's ICQ, Manders overlap, Jaccard, Dice) and as object-based measures
  (nearest-neighbour spot pairing, partner intensity sampled at detected spots),
  with spatial null models -- random-position, rotation, and translation nulls --
  that test whether apparent colocalization exceeds what co-distribution within
  the same compartment would produce by chance. The rotation null is intended
  for a diffuse partner channel, such as a nucleoplasm-filling RNA-binding
  protein, where no thresholdable object exists to mask.
